# Compliance & Regulatory Alignment

## Core Principle

Meet compliance requirements (SOC 2, NIST SSDF) by building auditable evidence directly into automated workflows, minimizing friction and manual effort.

---

## 1. SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria

### Principle

SOC 2 is based on five Trust Services Criteria. Generate evidence automatically from CI/CD pipelines instead of manually gathering documentation.

### Criteria Mapping

| SOC 2 Criteria | Automated Evidence from CI/CD |
|----------------|------------------------------|
| **Security (CC6)** | IAM policies (IaC), RBAC configurations, JIT access logs, MFA enforcement, secret scan results |
| **Availability (A1)** | SLO dashboards, error budget reports, postmortem action item tracking, rollback logs |
| **Change Management (CC3)** | Git commit history, PR approval records, pipeline execution logs, test results |
| **Processing Integrity (PI1)** | Input validation schemas, transaction logs, idempotency key usage logs |
| **Confidentiality** | Encryption at rest/transit evidence, access logs, data classification |

### Evidence Generation

- **Version Control**: Git history provides audit trail
- **Pipeline Logs**: CI/CD execution logs show controls in action
- **IaC**: Infrastructure as Code shows configuration at point in time
- **Test Results**: Automated test execution proves code quality
- **Scan Results**: Security scans show vulnerability management

### Rules

- ✅ **DO**: Generate evidence automatically from pipelines
- ✅ **DO**: Store evidence in version-controlled repositories
- ✅ **DO**: Map pipeline artifacts to SOC 2 criteria
- ✅ **DO**: Maintain audit trail for all changes
- ❌ **DON'T**: Generate evidence manually
- ❌ **DON'T**: Store evidence outside version control
- ❌ **DON'T**: Lose evidence after changes

---

## 2. Continuous Audit Readiness

### Principle

Maintain a state of continuous readiness by version-controlling policies and maintaining immutable evidence chains.

### Policies as Code

- Define security and compliance policies as code (OPA/Rego)
- Version control all policy files
- Review policies in pull requests
- Track policy changes over time

### Immutable Evidence Chain

- Trace production deployments back through pipeline logs
- Link to specific tests passed, policies enforced, approvals granted
- Connect to original source code commit
- Maintain timestamped revision history

### Process Maturity

- Complete, time-stamped revision history for all procedural updates
- Demonstrates process maturity to auditors
- Shows continuous improvement over time

### Rules

- ✅ **DO**: Define policies as code
- ✅ **DO**: Maintain immutable evidence chains
- ✅ **DO**: Track all changes with timestamps
- ✅ **DO**: Demonstrate process maturity
- ❌ **DON'T**: Maintain policies outside version control
- ❌ **DON'T**: Lose evidence of historical changes

---

## 3. Automated Evidence Collection

### Principle

Automate collection of compliance evidence to reduce manual effort and ensure consistency.

### Evidence Sources

- **Git Commits**: Code changes and approvals
- **CI/CD Logs**: Pipeline execution and test results
- **Security Scans**: Vulnerability and secret detection results
- **Infrastructure**: IaC configurations and deployments
- **Access Logs**: Authentication and authorization events

### Evidence Storage

- Store in version-controlled repository
- Organize by compliance framework and criteria
- Include metadata (timestamp, service, version)
- Make searchable and auditable

### Rules

- ✅ **DO**: Automate evidence collection
- ✅ **DO**: Store evidence in version control
- ✅ **DO**: Organize evidence by framework/criteria
- ✅ **DO**: Include metadata with evidence
- ❌ **DON'T**: Collect evidence manually
- ❌ **DON'T**: Store evidence in silos
- ❌ **DON'T**: Lose evidence over time

---

## 4. NIST SSDF Alignment

### Principle

Align practices with NIST Secure Software Development Framework (SSDF) for secure software development.

### SSDF Practices

- **PO.1**: Prepare the organization
- **PO.2**: Protect the software
- **PO.3**: Produce well-secured software
- **PO.4**: Respond to vulnerabilities

### Implementation

- Use secure development practices (input validation, least privilege)
- Implement security scanning (SAST, SCA, dependency scanning)
- Maintain software inventory (SBOMs)
- Track and respond to vulnerabilities

### Rules

- ✅ **DO**: Align with NIST SSDF practices
- ✅ **DO**: Implement secure development practices
- ✅ **DO**: Use security scanning tools
- ✅ **DO**: Maintain software inventory
- ❌ **DON'T**: Ignore SSDF alignment
- ❌ **DON'T**: Skip security practices

---

## 5. Audit Preparation

### Principle

Prepare for audits by maintaining organized, accessible evidence and documentation.

### Audit Readiness Checklist

- [ ] All policies defined as code
- [ ] Evidence automatically collected
- [ ] Evidence organized by framework/criteria
- [ ] Audit trail maintained for all changes
- [ ] Documentation up-to-date
- [ ] Evidence searchable and accessible

### Rules

- ✅ **DO**: Maintain audit readiness continuously
- ✅ **DO**: Organize evidence for easy access
- ✅ **DO**: Keep documentation current
- ✅ **DO**: Prepare audit packages in advance
- ❌ **DON'T**: Prepare for audits at the last minute
- ❌ **DON'T**: Scramble to find evidence during audit

---

